<sn><md>### History of Computer Networks

**Overview**

Computer networks evolved from `simple remote computer communication` in the 1940s into the `modern Internet` through the development of **modems, time-sharing, packet switching, ARPANET, TCP/IP, and Ethernet**.

**Main concept:** Computer networking developed progressively from remote computer communication and resource sharing into `interconnected networks` using packet switching, internetworking protocols, and high-speed Ethernet.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Early Origins (1940–1960s)

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/computer_history_early_origin.jpeg)

| Year | Event | Description |
|------|-------|-------------|
| 1940 | George Stibitz | Demonstrated the first real-time remote use of a computer by connecting a teletype at Dartmouth to a Bell Labs computer. |
| 1950s | SAGE | The U.S. military developed a computer network for its radar system using the Bell 101 modem. |
| 1958 | Bell 101 Modem | AT&amp;T released the first commercial computer modem, transmitting digital data over telephone lines at 110 bits per second. |
| 1959 | Time-sharing | Christopher Strachey and John McCarthy contributed to the development of time-sharing, allowing multiple users to share computing resources. |
| 1960s | Packet switching | Paul Baran and Donald Davies independently developed the concept of packet switching for computer data communication. |
| 1968–1969 | NPL Network | The National Physical Laboratory implemented packet switching in a local area network using 768 kbit/s links. |

**Time-Sharing (1959):** A technique where multiple users share one computer simultaneously. The CPU rapidly switches between users' tasks, giving each the illusion of dedicated access. It made computing interactive and cost-effective.

**Packet Switching (1960s):** A method of sending data by breaking it into small packets that travel independently across a network and are reassembled at the destination. It is efficient, resilient, and the foundation of the Internet.</md></sn><sn><md>### ARPANET (1969–1974)

**First Four Nodes (1969)**

- University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA)
- Stanford Research Institute
- University of California, Santa Barbara (UCSB)
- University of Utah

Key Developments

| Year | Event | Description |
|------|-------|-------------|
| — | Leonard Kleinrock | Contributed mathematical research on packet-switched network performance. |
| 1973 | Internetworking | Peter Kirstein connected ARPANET to British academic networks, creating the first international heterogeneous computer network. |
| 1973 | Ethernet | Robert Metcalfe and David Boggs developed Ethernet, a local area networking system. |
| 1973 | CYCLADES | The French network promoted reliable data delivery by the host computers rather than relying solely on the network. |

</md></sn><sn><md>### Development of the Internet (1974–Present)

| Year | Event | Description |
|------|-------|-------------|
| 1974 | TCP/IP foundations | Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn published research on internetworking. The first TCP specification was written later that year. |
| 1976 | Ethernet research | Metcalfe and Boggs published a paper on Ethernet and distributed packet switching. |
| 1976 | ARCNET | John Murphy created ARCNET, a token-passing network. |
| 1980 | Ethernet | Ethernet speed was upgraded to 10 Mbit/s. |
| 1986 | NSFNET | The National Science Foundation launched a research network connecting research and educational institutions. |
| 1995 | Ethernet | Ethernet speeds increased from 10 Mbit/s to 100 Mbit/s. |
| 1998 | Ethernet | Ethernet supported speeds of 1 Gbit/s. |
| Modern era | — | Ethernet continued increasing in speed, while network protocols expanded to support embedded systems, ubiquitous computing, and the Internet of Things (IoT). |
</md></sn><sn><md>### Commercial Use of Computer Networks

| Year | Event | Description |
|------|-------|-------------|
| 1960 | SABRE | A commercial airline reservation system went online using two connected mainframes. |
| 1965 | Western Electric | Introduced a widely used computer-controlled telephone switch. |
| 1972 | Public data networks | Commercial services began operating on experimental public data networks in Europe. |
| Late 1970s | X.25 and X.75 | Public data networks adopted X.25 and interconnected using X.75. |
| 1977 | Fiber network | GTE deployed its first long-distance fiber network in Long Beach, California. |
| 1980s | TCP/IP expansion | The infrastructure of public data networks supported the expansion of TCP/IP networks. |
